+/// Length of the header prepended to every encoded frame: one flags byte, then
+/// the capture timestamp as a little-endian u64 of microseconds.
+const HEADER_LEN: usize = 9;
+
+/// Flags bit 0: the frame that follows begins with an IDR. The client cannot
+/// decode anything before the first one.
+const FLAG_KEYFRAME: u8 = 1;
+
+/// Epoch of the capture timestamps on the wire: the first frame this process
+/// encodes. `Instant` is monotonic, so the timestamps never run backwards, and
+/// unlike the encoder's own start time this one survives the encoder being torn
+/// down and rebuilt when the capture size changes. There is no way to convert
+/// it to a client's clock; the client measures the offset against its own.
+static CAPTURE_EPOCH: LazyLock<Instant> = LazyLock::new(Instant::now);
